// Each paper: 100 marks, 2 hours, calculator allowed. Data used: g = 9.81 N kg⁻¹, R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹, N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹,
// u = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, 1 u ≡ 931.5 MeV, 1 MeV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹³ J. Points are PHYS topic ids followed by spec outcome codes.

BCSP.add({
  id: 'a21-easy', unit: 'a21', level: 'easy',
  title: 'Physics A2 1 · Paper A (Easy)', minutes: 120, calc: true,
  questions: [
    { n: 1, points: ['solids', '4.1.1', '4.1.2', '4.1.3', '4.1.4', '4.1.6'],
      q: 'A steel wire of original length 2.00 m and diameter 0.50 mm hangs vertically. A load of 40 N is attached to its lower end and the wire extends by 2.0 mm. The wire obeys Hooke’s law.',
      parts: [
        { l: 'a', marks: 2, q: 'State Hooke’s law.',
          answer: 'The extension of a spring or wire is directly proportional to the applied force (load), provided the limit of proportionality is not exceeded.',
          ms: ['B1 extension directly proportional to force / load', 'B1 provided the limit of proportionality is not exceeded'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Hooke’s law links the force on a wire or spring to how much it stretches.', show: 'force $\\leftrightarrow$ extension' },
            { say: 'The extension is directly proportional to the force applied.', show: '$F = kx$' },
            { say: 'This only holds up to the limit of proportionality, so you must say that for the second mark.', show: '…provided the limit of proportionality is not exceeded' } ] },
        { l: 'b', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the cross-sectional area of the wire.',
          answer: '$1.96 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\text{m}^2$',
          ms: ['C1 $A = \\pi r^2$ with $r = 0.25 \\times 10^{-3}$ m', 'A1 $1.96 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\text{m}^2$'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'The cross-section is a circle, so we need the radius, which is half the diameter.', show: '$r = 0.25\\,\\text{mm} = 0.25 \\times 10^{-3}\\,\\text{m}$' },
            { say: 'Area equals pi r squared.', show: '$A = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (0.25 \\times 10^{-3})^2$' },
            { say: 'That gives about one point nine six times ten to the minus seven square metres.', show: '$A = 1.96 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\text{m}^2$' } ] },
        { l: 'c', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the tensile stress in the wire.',
          answer: '$2.04 \\times 10^{8}\\,\\text{Pa}$',
          ms: ['C1 $\\sigma = F/A$', 'A1 $2.04 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa (ecf from (b))'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Stress is force divided by cross-sectional area.', show: '$\\sigma = \\dfrac{F}{A}$' },
            { say: 'Put in forty newtons and the area from part b.', show: '$\\sigma = \\dfrac{40}{1.96 \\times 10^{-7}}$' },
            { say: 'The stress is about two point zero four times ten to the eight pascals.', show: '$\\sigma = 2.04 \\times 10^{8}\\,\\text{Pa}$' } ] },
        { l: 'd', marks: 1, q: 'Calculate the tensile strain.',
          answer: '$1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ (no unit)',
          ms: ['B1 $1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$, no unit'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Strain is extension divided by original length, with both in metres.', show: '$\\varepsilon = \\dfrac{x}{L} = \\dfrac{2.0 \\times 10^{-3}}{2.00}$' },
            { say: 'That is one times ten to the minus three.', show: '$\\varepsilon = 1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$' },
            { say: 'Strain is a ratio of two lengths, so it has no unit.', show: 'no unit' } ] },
        { l: 'e', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the Young modulus of the steel.',
          answer: '$2.04 \\times 10^{11}\\,\\text{Pa}$',
          ms: ['C1 $E = \\sigma/\\varepsilon$', 'A1 $2.04 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa (ecf)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'The Young modulus is stress divided by strain.', show: '$E = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon}$' },
            { say: 'Use the answers to parts c and d.', show: '$E = \\dfrac{2.04 \\times 10^{8}}{1.0 \\times 10^{-3}}$' },
            { say: 'So the Young modulus is about two point zero four times ten to the eleven pascals, typical for steel.', show: '$E = 2.04 \\times 10^{11}\\,\\text{Pa}$' } ] },
        { l: 'f', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the strain energy stored in the wire.',
          answer: '$0.040\\,\\text{J}$',
          ms: ['C1 $E_p = \\tfrac{1}{2}Fx$', 'A1 0.040 J'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'For a wire obeying Hooke’s law, the stored energy is half the force times the extension.', show: '$E_p = \\tfrac{1}{2}Fx$' },
            { say: 'Remember to convert two millimetres to metres.', show: '$E_p = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 40 \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$' },
            { say: 'The energy stored is zero point zero four joules.', show: '$E_p = 0.040\\,\\text{J}$' } ] },
        { l: 'g', marks: 2, q: 'Explain the difference between elastic and plastic deformation.',
          answer: 'Elastic: the material returns to its original shape and size when the load is removed. Plastic: it does not; there is a permanent deformation.',
          ms: ['B1 elastic: returns to original length/shape when load removed', 'B1 plastic: permanent deformation / does not return'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Think about what happens when the load is taken off.', show: 'load removed $\\Rightarrow$ ?' },
            { say: 'In elastic deformation the wire goes back to its original length.', show: 'Elastic: returns to original length' },
            { say: 'In plastic deformation it stays stretched: the change is permanent.', show: 'Plastic: permanent extension remains' } ] },
        { l: 'h', marks: 1, q: 'State what is meant by the elastic limit.',
          answer: 'The greatest stress (or load) that can be applied and the material still returns to its original length when the load is removed.',
          ms: ['B1 maximum load/stress for which the material returns to its original dimensions'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'The elastic limit is the boundary between elastic and plastic behaviour.', show: 'elastic $\\to$ plastic' },
            { say: 'It is the largest load for which the wire still returns to its original length.', show: 'max load with full recovery' },
            { say: 'Note it is not the same as the limit of proportionality, which is where Hooke’s law stops.', show: 'elastic limit $\\neq$ limit of proportionality' } ] } ] },

    { n: 2, points: ['thermal', '4.2.2', '4.2.3', '4.2.4', '4.2.6', '4.2.7'],
      q: 'A sealed container of fixed volume $12.0\\,\\text{dm}^3$ holds 0.50 mol of an ideal gas at 27 °C.',
      parts: [
        { l: 'a', marks: 3, q: 'Calculate the pressure of the gas.',
          answer: '$1.04 \\times 10^{5}\\,\\text{Pa}$',
          ms: ['C1 $T = 300$ K', 'C1 $V = 0.0120\\,\\text{m}^3$ and $p = nRT/V$', 'A1 $1.04 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Gas law calculations always need the temperature in kelvin, so add two hundred and seventy three.', show: '$T = 27 + 273 = 300\\,\\text{K}$' },
            { say: 'Convert the volume to cubic metres. One cubic decimetre is ten to the minus three cubic metres.', show: '$V = 12.0 \\times 10^{-3}\\,\\text{m}^3$' },
            { say: 'Rearrange p V equals n R T for the pressure.', show: '$p = \\dfrac{nRT}{V} = \\dfrac{0.50 \\times 8.31 \\times 300}{0.0120}$' },
            { say: 'The pressure is about one point zero four times ten to the five pascals.', show: '$p = 1.04 \\times 10^{5}\\,\\text{Pa}$' } ] },
        { l: 'b', marks: 1, q: 'Calculate the number of molecules in the container.',
          answer: '$3.01 \\times 10^{23}$',
          ms: ['B1 $N = nN_A = 3.01 \\times 10^{23}$'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'The number of molecules is the number of moles times the Avogadro constant.', show: '$N = nN_A$' },
            { say: 'Multiply nought point five by six point nought two times ten to the twenty three.', show: '$N = 0.50 \\times 6.02 \\times 10^{23}$' },
            { say: 'That is three point nought one times ten to the twenty three molecules.', show: '$N = 3.01 \\times 10^{23}$' } ] },
        { l: 'c', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the mean translational kinetic energy of a gas molecule.',
          answer: '$6.21 \\times 10^{-21}\\,\\text{J}$',
          ms: ['C1 $\\tfrac{1}{2}m\\langle c^2\\rangle = \\tfrac{3}{2}kT$', 'A1 $6.21 \\times 10^{-21}$ J'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'The mean kinetic energy of a molecule depends only on the kelvin temperature.', show: '$\\tfrac{1}{2}m\\langle c^2\\rangle = \\tfrac{3}{2}kT$' },
            { say: 'Substitute the Boltzmann constant and three hundred kelvin.', show: '$= 1.5 \\times 1.38 \\times 10^{-23} \\times 300$' },
            { say: 'This gives six point two one times ten to the minus twenty one joules.', show: '$= 6.21 \\times 10^{-21}\\,\\text{J}$' } ] },
        { l: 'd', marks: 2, q: 'The gas is heated to 127 °C. The volume does not change. Calculate the new pressure.',
          answer: '$1.39 \\times 10^{5}\\,\\text{Pa}$',
          ms: ['C1 $p/T$ constant with $T_2 = 400$ K', 'A1 $1.39 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa (ecf)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'At constant volume, pressure divided by kelvin temperature stays the same.', show: '$\\dfrac{p_1}{T_1} = \\dfrac{p_2}{T_2}$' },
            { say: 'One hundred and twenty seven degrees Celsius is four hundred kelvin.', show: '$T_2 = 127 + 273 = 400\\,\\text{K}$' },
            { say: 'Scale the pressure by four hundred over three hundred.', show: '$p_2 = 1.04 \\times 10^{5} \\times \\dfrac{400}{300}$' },
            { say: 'The new pressure is about one point three nine times ten to the five pascals.', show: '$p_2 = 1.39 \\times 10^{5}\\,\\text{Pa}$' } ] },
        { l: 'e', marks: 3, q: 'Explain, in terms of the motion of the molecules, why the pressure increases when the temperature rises at constant volume.',
          answer: 'Higher temperature means greater mean kinetic energy, so molecules move faster. They hit the walls more often and each collision involves a bigger change of momentum, so the force per unit area on the walls is larger.',
          ms: ['B1 mean KE / speed of molecules increases', 'B1 collisions with walls more frequent', 'B1 greater change of momentum per collision, so greater force (per unit area)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Start with what temperature means: it measures the average kinetic energy of the molecules.', show: '$T \\uparrow \\Rightarrow \\langle E_k \\rangle \\uparrow$' },
            { say: 'Faster molecules cross the container more quickly, so they hit the walls more often.', show: 'more collisions per second' },
            { say: 'Each collision also involves a bigger change in momentum.', show: '$\\Delta p$ per collision $\\uparrow$' },
            { say: 'Force is rate of change of momentum, so the force on each square metre of wall, the pressure, increases.', show: '$F = \\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t} \\uparrow \\Rightarrow p = \\dfrac{F}{A} \\uparrow$' } ] },
        { l: 'f', marks: 2, q: 'State what is meant by the internal energy of a gas.',
          answer: 'The sum of the random kinetic and potential energies of all the molecules in the gas.',
          ms: ['B1 sum / total of kinetic and potential energies', 'B1 of all the molecules, randomly distributed'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Internal energy is a total for the whole sample, not one molecule.', show: 'total over all molecules' },
            { say: 'It includes the kinetic energy of the molecules’ random motion and the potential energy from forces between them.', show: '$U = \\sum (E_k + E_p)$' },
            { say: 'For an ideal gas the potential energy part is zero, so internal energy is all kinetic.', show: 'ideal gas: $U = \\sum E_k$' } ] },
        { l: 'g', marks: 1, q: 'State what is meant by absolute zero.',
          answer: 'The lowest possible temperature, 0 K (−273 °C), at which the molecules have minimum kinetic energy.',
          ms: ['B1 temperature at which molecules have minimum (zero) kinetic energy / 0 K = −273 °C'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Absolute zero is zero on the kelvin scale.', show: '$0\\,\\text{K} = -273\\,{}^\\circ\\text{C}$' },
            { say: 'It is the temperature at which the molecules have the least possible kinetic energy.', show: 'minimum molecular kinetic energy' },
            { say: 'Nothing can be colder, which is why it is called absolute.', show: 'lowest possible temperature' } ] } ] },

    { n: 3, points: ['thermal', '4.2.8', '4.2.9'],
      q: 'A student uses an electrical method to find the specific heat capacity of aluminium. A 12.0 V heater draws a current of 4.0 A for 300 s and warms a 1.00 kg aluminium block from 18.0 °C to 34.0 °C.',
      parts: [
        { l: 'a', marks: 1, q: 'Define specific heat capacity.',
          answer: 'The energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K (1 °C).',
          ms: ['B1 energy per kg per K (to raise temperature of 1 kg by 1 K)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Specific means per kilogram.', show: 'per 1 kg' },
            { say: 'It is the energy needed to raise one kilogram by one kelvin.', show: 'energy to raise 1 kg by 1 K' },
            { say: 'Its unit is joules per kilogram per kelvin.', show: '$\\text{J kg}^{-1}\\,\\text{K}^{-1}$' } ] },
        { l: 'b', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the electrical energy supplied by the heater.',
          answer: '14 400 J',
          ms: ['C1 $E = VIt$', 'A1 14 400 J'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Electrical energy is voltage times current times time.', show: '$E = VIt$' },
            { say: 'Substitute twelve volts, four amps and three hundred seconds.', show: '$E = 12.0 \\times 4.0 \\times 300$' },
            { say: 'That is fourteen thousand four hundred joules.', show: '$E = 14\\,400\\,\\text{J}$' } ] },
        { l: 'c', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the specific heat capacity of aluminium given by these results.',
          answer: '$900\\,\\text{J kg}^{-1}\\,\\text{K}^{-1}$',
          ms: ['C1 $c = Q/(m\\Delta\\theta)$ with $\\Delta\\theta = 16.0$ K', 'A1 900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'The temperature rise is thirty four minus eighteen, which is sixteen kelvin.', show: '$\\Delta\\theta = 34.0 - 18.0 = 16.0\\,\\text{K}$' },
            { say: 'Rearrange Q equals m c delta theta for c.', show: '$c = \\dfrac{Q}{m\\Delta\\theta} = \\dfrac{14\\,400}{1.00 \\times 16.0}$' },
            { say: 'So c is nine hundred joules per kilogram per kelvin.', show: '$c = 900\\,\\text{J kg}^{-1}\\,\\text{K}^{-1}$' } ] },
        { l: 'd', marks: 2, q: 'In practice some energy is lost to the surroundings. State and explain the effect this has on the value of $c$ obtained.',
          answer: 'The calculated $c$ is too large: not all of the energy supplied goes into the block, so the temperature rise is smaller than it would be, and $c = Q/(m\\Delta\\theta)$ is overestimated.',
          ms: ['B1 value of c too high / overestimate', 'B1 temperature rise smaller than if no losses (for the same Q)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'If heat escapes, the block gets less energy than the heater supplies.', show: 'energy to block $<$ $VIt$' },
            { say: 'So the temperature rise is smaller than it should be.', show: '$\\Delta\\theta$ too small' },
            { say: 'Dividing the full energy by a temperature rise that is too small makes c too big.', show: '$c = \\dfrac{Q}{m\\Delta\\theta}$ too large' } ] },
        { l: 'e', marks: 2, q: 'Suggest two practical steps that reduce errors in this experiment.',
          answer: 'Any two: insulate (lag) the block; put a drop of oil in the thermometer and heater holes for good thermal contact; start below room temperature and finish the same amount above it; record the highest temperature reached after switching off.',
          ms: ['B1 first valid step (e.g. lag the block)', 'B1 second valid step (e.g. oil in holes for thermal contact)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'The main error is heat loss, so wrap the block in insulation.', show: '1. lag / insulate the block' },
            { say: 'Poor contact between heater, thermometer and block also causes errors, so add a drop of oil in the holes.', show: '2. oil in holes for good thermal contact' },
            { say: 'Other good answers include reading the highest temperature reached after switching off the heater.', show: 'also: record maximum temperature after switch-off' } ] } ] },

    { n: 4, points: ['circular', '4.3.1', '4.3.2', '4.3.3'],
      q: 'A ball of mass 0.20 kg is attached to a light string and moves in a horizontal circle of radius 0.60 m on a smooth table. It makes 2.5 revolutions every second.',
      parts: [
        { l: 'a', marks: 1, q: 'State what is meant by angular velocity.',
          answer: 'The angle swept out by the radius per unit time (rate of change of angle), in rad s⁻¹.',
          ms: ['B1 angle (in radians) swept per second / rate of change of angular displacement'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'As the ball goes round, the string sweeps out an angle.', show: 'angle $\\theta$ swept by radius' },
            { say: 'Angular velocity is how fast that angle changes.', show: '$\\omega = \\dfrac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t}$' },
            { say: 'It is measured in radians per second.', show: '$\\text{rad s}^{-1}$' } ] },
        { l: 'b', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the angular velocity of the ball.',
          answer: '$15.7\\,\\text{rad s}^{-1}$',
          ms: ['C1 $\\omega = 2\\pi f$', 'A1 15.7 rad s⁻¹'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Each revolution is two pi radians, and there are two point five revolutions per second.', show: '$\\omega = 2\\pi f$' },
            { say: 'Multiply two pi by two point five.', show: '$\\omega = 2\\pi \\times 2.5$' },
            { say: 'The angular velocity is about fifteen point seven radians per second.', show: '$\\omega = 15.7\\,\\text{rad s}^{-1}$' } ] },
        { l: 'c', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the speed of the ball.',
          answer: '$9.42\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$',
          ms: ['C1 $v = r\\omega$', 'A1 9.42 m s⁻¹ (ecf)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Linear speed is radius times angular velocity.', show: '$v = r\\omega$' },
            { say: 'Use nought point six metres and the angular velocity just found.', show: '$v = 0.60 \\times 15.7$' },
            { say: 'The speed is about nine point four two metres per second.', show: '$v = 9.42\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$' } ] },
        { l: 'd', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the centripetal acceleration of the ball.',
          answer: '$148\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$',
          ms: ['C1 $a = v^2/r$ or $r\\omega^2$', 'A1 148 m s⁻² (ecf)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Centripetal acceleration can be written as r omega squared.', show: '$a = r\\omega^2$' },
            { say: 'Substitute the radius and angular velocity.', show: '$a = 0.60 \\times 15.7^2$' },
            { say: 'That is about one hundred and forty eight metres per second squared, towards the centre.', show: '$a = 148\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$' } ] },
        { l: 'e', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the tension in the string.',
          answer: '29.6 N',
          ms: ['C1 $F = ma$ (= $mr\\omega^2$)', 'A1 29.6 N (ecf)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'The tension is the only horizontal force, so it provides the centripetal force.', show: '$T = ma$' },
            { say: 'Multiply the mass by the centripetal acceleration.', show: '$T = 0.20 \\times 148$' },
            { say: 'The tension is about twenty nine point six newtons.', show: '$T = 29.6\\,\\text{N}$' } ] },
        { l: 'f', marks: 2, q: 'The speed of the ball is constant. Explain why the ball is accelerating.',
          answer: 'Velocity is a vector; the direction of motion is continually changing, so the velocity changes and there is an acceleration (towards the centre).',
          ms: ['B1 velocity is a vector / direction is changing', 'B1 change of velocity means acceleration (directed to centre)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Speed is constant, but velocity includes direction.', show: 'velocity = speed + direction' },
            { say: 'Moving in a circle, the direction changes all the time.', show: 'direction changes continuously' },
            { say: 'A changing velocity means an acceleration, and here it points to the centre of the circle.', show: '$a$ towards centre' } ] },
        { l: 'g', marks: 1, q: 'The string breaks. Describe the subsequent motion of the ball on the table.',
          answer: 'It moves off in a straight line at constant speed along the tangent to the circle at the point where the string broke.',
          ms: ['B1 straight line along the tangent (at constant speed)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Once the string breaks, there is no longer a resultant horizontal force.', show: '$F = 0$' },
            { say: 'By Newton’s first law the ball keeps its velocity at that instant.', show: 'constant velocity' },
            { say: 'So it travels in a straight line along the tangent.', show: 'straight line, tangent to circle' } ] } ] },

    { n: 5, points: ['shm', '4.4.1', '4.4.2', '4.4.4', '4.4.6', '4.4.8'],
      parts: [
        { l: 'a', marks: 2, q: 'Define simple harmonic motion.',
          answer: 'Motion in which the acceleration is directly proportional to the displacement from a fixed point and always directed towards that point ($a = -\\omega^2 x$).',
          ms: ['B1 acceleration proportional to displacement (from equilibrium position)', 'B1 directed towards the equilibrium position / opposite to displacement'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Simple harmonic motion is defined by how the acceleration depends on displacement.', show: '$a \\propto x$' },
            { say: 'The acceleration is directly proportional to the displacement from the equilibrium position.', show: 'proportional to displacement' },
            { say: 'And it always points back towards the equilibrium position, which is what the minus sign shows.', show: '$a = -\\omega^2 x$' } ] },
        { l: 'b', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the period of a simple pendulum of length 0.80 m.',
          answer: '1.79 s',
          ms: ['C1 $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{l/g}$', 'A1 1.79 s'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'For a simple pendulum, the period depends only on length and g.', show: '$T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}}$' },
            { say: 'Substitute nought point eight metres and nine point eight one.', show: '$T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.80}{9.81}}$' },
            { say: 'The period is about one point seven nine seconds.', show: '$T = 1.79\\,\\text{s}$' } ] },
        { l: 'c', marks: 8, q: 'A mass of 0.40 kg hangs on a spring of spring constant 25 N m⁻¹. It is pulled down 3.0 cm and released at $t = 0$, so that $x = A\\cos\\omega t$.<br>(i) Calculate the period. [2]<br>(ii) Calculate the angular frequency $\\omega$. [1]<br>(iii) Calculate the maximum speed. [2]<br>(iv) Calculate the maximum acceleration. [1]<br>(v) Calculate the displacement at $t = 0.10$ s. [2]',
          answer: '(i) 0.795 s (ii) 7.91 rad s⁻¹ (iii) 0.237 m s⁻¹ (iv) 1.88 m s⁻² (v) 2.1 cm (0.021 m)',
          ms: ['(i) C1 $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$; A1 0.795 s', '(ii) B1 $\\omega = 2\\pi/T = 7.91$ rad s⁻¹', '(iii) C1 $v_{max} = A\\omega$; A1 0.237 m s⁻¹', '(iv) B1 $a_{max} = A\\omega^2 = 1.88$ m s⁻²', '(v) C1 $x = 0.030\\cos(7.91 \\times 0.10)$ in radians; A1 0.021 m'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'For a mass on a spring, the period is two pi root m over k.', show: '(i) $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.40}{25}} = 0.795\\,\\text{s}$' },
            { say: 'Angular frequency is two pi over the period.', show: '(ii) $\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{0.795} = 7.91\\,\\text{rad s}^{-1}$' },
            { say: 'Maximum speed is amplitude times omega. Use the amplitude in metres.', show: '(iii) $v_{max} = 0.030 \\times 7.91 = 0.237\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$' },
            { say: 'Maximum acceleration is amplitude times omega squared.', show: '(iv) $a_{max} = 0.030 \\times 7.91^2 = 1.88\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$' },
            { say: 'For the displacement, put t equals nought point one into x equals A cos omega t, with the calculator in radians.', show: '(v) $x = 0.030\\cos(0.791) = 0.030 \\times 0.703$' },
            { say: 'The displacement is about nought point nought two one metres, or two point one centimetres.', show: '$x = 0.021\\,\\text{m}$' } ] },
        { l: 'd', marks: 3, q: 'Explain what is meant by resonance and give one mechanical example where resonance is a problem.',
          answer: 'Resonance: when a system is forced to vibrate at a frequency equal to its natural frequency, the amplitude becomes a maximum (energy is transferred most efficiently). Example: a bridge swaying when people march across in step / vibrating panels in a car at a certain engine speed.',
          ms: ['B1 driving (forcing) frequency equals natural frequency', 'B1 amplitude of oscillation is a maximum', 'B1 valid example where it is a problem'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Resonance happens in forced vibrations.', show: 'a periodic driving force acts' },
            { say: 'When the driving frequency equals the natural frequency of the system…', show: '$f_{driving} = f_{natural}$' },
            { say: '…the amplitude grows to a maximum because energy is transferred most efficiently.', show: 'amplitude maximum' },
            { say: 'A good example of a problem is a footbridge that sways when people walk in step at its natural frequency.', show: 'e.g. footbridge, car panels rattling' } ] } ] },

    { n: 6, points: ['nucleus', 'decay', '4.5.1', '4.5.3', '4.6.2'],
      parts: [
        { l: 'a', marks: 4, q: 'In the alpha-particle scattering experiment, alpha particles were fired at thin gold foil. State two observations and the conclusion drawn from each.',
          answer: 'Most alpha particles passed straight through: the atom is mostly empty space. A very small number were deflected through large angles (more than 90°): there is a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus containing most of the mass.',
          ms: ['B1 most pass straight through (undeflected)', 'B1 so atom is mostly empty space', 'B1 a few deflected through large angles / back-scattered', 'B1 so mass and positive charge concentrated in a tiny nucleus'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'First observation: nearly all the alpha particles went straight through the foil.', show: 'most undeflected' },
            { say: 'That means the atom must be mostly empty space.', show: '$\\Rightarrow$ atom mostly empty space' },
            { say: 'Second observation: a tiny fraction bounced back through large angles.', show: 'very few deflected $> 90^\\circ$' },
            { say: 'Only a very small, dense, positive centre could repel them so strongly. That is the nucleus.', show: '$\\Rightarrow$ small, dense, positive nucleus' } ] },
        { l: 'b', marks: 2, q: 'Using $R = r_0A^{1/3}$ with $r_0 = 1.2$ fm, calculate the radius of an iron-56 nucleus.',
          answer: '$4.6 \\times 10^{-15}\\,\\text{m}$ (4.59 fm)',
          ms: ['C1 $R = 1.2 \\times 10^{-15} \\times 56^{1/3}$', 'A1 $4.59 \\times 10^{-15}$ m'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'A is the nucleon number, fifty six.', show: '$A = 56$' },
            { say: 'Take the cube root of fifty six, which is about three point eight three.', show: '$56^{1/3} = 3.83$' },
            { say: 'Multiply by r nought, one point two femtometres.', show: '$R = 1.2 \\times 10^{-15} \\times 3.83$' },
            { say: 'The radius is about four point six times ten to the minus fifteen metres.', show: '$R = 4.59 \\times 10^{-15}\\,\\text{m}$' } ] },
        { l: 'c', marks: 2, q: 'Radon-222 ($Z = 86$) decays by alpha emission to an isotope of polonium. State the nucleon number and proton number of the polonium nucleus.',
          answer: 'Nucleon number 218, proton number 84',
          ms: ['B1 nucleon number 218', 'B1 proton number 84'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'An alpha particle is a helium nucleus: two protons and two neutrons.', show: '${}^{4}_{2}\\alpha$' },
            { say: 'The nucleon number falls by four.', show: '$222 - 4 = 218$' },
            { say: 'The proton number falls by two.', show: '$86 - 2 = 84$' },
            { say: 'So the daughter is polonium two one eight.', show: '${}^{222}_{86}\\text{Rn} \\to {}^{218}_{84}\\text{Po} + {}^{4}_{2}\\alpha$' } ] } ] },

    { n: 7, points: ['decay', '4.6.3', '4.6.4', '4.6.5', '4.6.7', '4.6.8'],
      q: 'Iodine-131 has a half-life of 8.0 days. A sample has an initial activity of $5.0 \\times 10^{6}$ Bq.',
      parts: [
        { l: 'a', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the decay constant of iodine-131 in s⁻¹.',
          answer: '$1.0 \\times 10^{-6}\\,\\text{s}^{-1}$',
          ms: ['C1 $\\lambda = 0.693/t_{1/2}$ with $t_{1/2}$ in seconds', 'A1 $1.00 \\times 10^{-6}$ s⁻¹'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Convert the half-life to seconds: eight days times eighty six thousand four hundred seconds per day.', show: '$t_{1/2} = 8.0 \\times 86\\,400 = 6.91 \\times 10^{5}\\,\\text{s}$' },
            { say: 'The decay constant is nought point six nine three divided by the half-life.', show: '$\\lambda = \\dfrac{0.693}{6.91 \\times 10^{5}}$' },
            { say: 'That is about one point nought times ten to the minus six per second.', show: '$\\lambda = 1.00 \\times 10^{-6}\\,\\text{s}^{-1}$' } ] },
        { l: 'b', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the number of undecayed iodine-131 nuclei initially present.',
          answer: '$5.0 \\times 10^{12}$',
          ms: ['C1 $N = A/\\lambda$', 'A1 $4.99 \\times 10^{12}$ (ecf)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Activity equals decay constant times number of nuclei, ignoring the minus sign which just shows N falls.', show: '$A = \\lambda N$' },
            { say: 'So N is the activity divided by lambda.', show: '$N = \\dfrac{5.0 \\times 10^{6}}{1.00 \\times 10^{-6}}$' },
            { say: 'There are about five times ten to the twelve nuclei.', show: '$N = 4.99 \\times 10^{12}$' } ] },
        { l: 'c', marks: 2, q: 'Calculate the activity of the sample after 20 days.',
          answer: '$8.8 \\times 10^{5}\\,\\text{Bq}$',
          ms: ['C1 $A = A_0e^{-\\lambda t}$ with consistent units', 'A1 $8.84 \\times 10^{5}$ Bq'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Use the exponential decay equation.', show: '$A = A_0e^{-\\lambda t}$' },
            { say: 'Lambda times t has no unit; using days, lambda is nought point six nine three over eight per day.', show: '$\\lambda t = \\dfrac{0.693}{8.0} \\times 20 = 1.73$' },
            { say: 'e to the minus one point seven three is about nought point one seven seven.', show: '$A = 5.0 \\times 10^{6} \\times e^{-1.73}$' },
            { say: 'The activity is about eight point eight times ten to the five becquerels.', show: '$A = 8.84 \\times 10^{5}\\,\\text{Bq}$' } ] },
        { l: 'd', marks: 2, q: 'Radioactive decay is described as random and exponential. Explain what each term means.',
          answer: 'Random: it is impossible to predict which nucleus will decay next or when a particular nucleus will decay. Exponential: the rate of decay (activity) is proportional to the number of undecayed nuclei, so the number falls by the same fraction in equal time intervals.',
          ms: ['B1 random: cannot predict which nucleus decays or when', 'B1 exponential: activity proportional to N / same fraction decays in equal times'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Random means we can never say which nucleus will decay next, or when.', show: 'random: unpredictable for one nucleus' },
            { say: 'Exponential means the decay rate is proportional to how many nuclei are left.', show: '$A = \\lambda N$' },
            { say: 'So in equal time intervals, the same fraction of the remaining nuclei decays.', show: 'equal fractions in equal times' } ] },
        { l: 'e', marks: 2, q: 'When measuring the half-life of a source with a Geiger–Müller tube, explain how background radiation is allowed for.',
          answer: 'Measure the background count rate with the source removed (over a long time), then subtract it from every measured count rate to get the corrected count rate.',
          ms: ['B1 measure background count with source absent', 'B1 subtract from each reading'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'The tube detects radiation from the surroundings as well as the source.', show: 'measured = source + background' },
            { say: 'So first measure the count rate with the source taken away, over a long time for accuracy.', show: 'record background count rate' },
            { say: 'Subtract that background from every reading to get the corrected count rate.', show: 'corrected = measured − background' } ] } ] },

    { n: 8, points: ['energy', '4.7.1', '4.7.2', '4.7.3', '4.7.4'],
      q: 'Mass of a helium-4 nucleus = 4.001506 u. Mass of a proton = 1.007276 u. Mass of a neutron = 1.008665 u. 1 u ≡ 931.5 MeV.',
      parts: [
        { l: 'a', marks: 2, q: 'Define the binding energy of a nucleus.',
          answer: 'The energy needed to separate a nucleus completely into its individual protons and neutrons (equivalently, the energy released when it forms from them).',
          ms: ['B1 energy required to separate (pull apart) the nucleus', 'B1 into its individual / separate nucleons'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Binding energy is about pulling a nucleus apart.', show: 'nucleus $\\to$ separate nucleons' },
            { say: 'It is the energy needed to separate the nucleus completely into its protons and neutrons.', show: 'energy required to separate all nucleons' },
            { say: 'It is equivalent to the mass defect times c squared.', show: '$E = \\Delta m c^2$' } ] },
        { l: 'b', marks: 5, q: 'For a helium-4 nucleus, calculate<br>(i) the mass defect in u, [2]<br>(ii) the binding energy in MeV, [2]<br>(iii) the binding energy per nucleon. [1]',
          answer: '(i) 0.030376 u (ii) 28.3 MeV (iii) 7.07 MeV per nucleon',
          ms: ['(i) C1 $2m_p + 2m_n - m_{He}$; A1 0.030376 u', '(ii) C1 $\\times 931.5$; A1 28.3 MeV', '(iii) B1 7.07 MeV (ecf)'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Helium four has two protons and two neutrons. Add their masses.', show: '$2(1.007276) + 2(1.008665) = 4.031882\\,\\text{u}$' },
            { say: 'Subtract the mass of the nucleus to get the mass defect.', show: '(i) $\\Delta m = 4.031882 - 4.001506 = 0.030376\\,\\text{u}$' },
            { say: 'Each atomic mass unit is equivalent to nine hundred and thirty one point five mega electron volts.', show: '(ii) $E = 0.030376 \\times 931.5 = 28.3\\,\\text{MeV}$' },
            { say: 'Divide by four nucleons for the binding energy per nucleon.', show: '(iii) $\\dfrac{28.3}{4} = 7.07\\,\\text{MeV}$' } ] },
        { l: 'c', marks: 2, q: 'Describe how the binding energy per nucleon varies with nucleon number.',
          answer: 'It rises steeply for light nuclei, reaches a maximum of about 8.8 MeV near iron-56 (A ≈ 56), then decreases slowly for heavier nuclei.',
          ms: ['B1 rises rapidly for small A to a peak near A = 56 (iron) of about 8.8 MeV', 'B1 then decreases gradually for large A'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'For the lightest nuclei the binding energy per nucleon is small and rises steeply.', show: 'light nuclei: low, rising fast' },
            { say: 'It peaks at about eight point eight mega electron volts around iron fifty six, the most stable nucleus.', show: 'maximum $\\approx 8.8$ MeV at $A \\approx 56$' },
            { say: 'After that it falls slowly for heavier nuclei like uranium.', show: 'heavy nuclei: gradual decrease ($\\approx 7.6$ MeV for U)' } ] },
        { l: 'd', marks: 3, q: 'Use the binding energy per nucleon curve to explain why both the fission of heavy nuclei and the fusion of light nuclei release energy.',
          answer: 'In both processes the products have a greater binding energy per nucleon than the original nuclei (they move towards the peak at iron). The total binding energy increases, so the products have less mass and the mass difference is released as energy.',
          ms: ['B1 fission: heavy nucleus splits into nuclei of higher BE per nucleon', 'B1 fusion: light nuclei join to give higher BE per nucleon', 'B1 increase in binding energy / loss of mass released as energy'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Energy is released when nucleons end up more tightly bound, that is, higher up the curve.', show: 'higher BE per nucleon $\\Rightarrow$ energy released' },
            { say: 'Splitting a heavy nucleus like uranium gives middle-sized nuclei nearer the peak.', show: 'fission: U $\\to$ medium nuclei, move up curve' },
            { say: 'Joining light nuclei like hydrogen isotopes gives helium, also higher up the curve.', show: 'fusion: H $\\to$ He, move up curve' },
            { say: 'In both cases the total binding energy increases, the mass decreases, and the lost mass appears as energy.', show: '$E = \\Delta m c^2$' } ] } ] },

    { n: 9, points: ['fusion', '4.8.1'],
      parts: [
        { l: 'a', marks: 6, q: 'Describe how a thermal fission reactor is controlled and made safe. Your answer should refer to the chain reaction, the moderator, the control rods, the cooling system and the shielding. <i>In this question you will be assessed on your written communication skills.</i>',
          answer: 'Fission of U-235 releases 2–3 neutrons which cause further fissions (chain reaction). A moderator (e.g. graphite or water) slows fast neutrons by collisions so they are more likely to cause fission. Control rods (boron or cadmium) absorb neutrons so that on average one neutron per fission causes another fission; lowering them slows or stops the reaction. A coolant (water or CO₂) is pumped through the core to remove heat to the boiler/turbines and stop overheating. Thick steel and concrete shielding absorbs neutrons and gamma radiation to protect workers.',
          ms: ['Indicative content: chain reaction — each fission releases 2–3 neutrons that can cause further fission; moderator slows neutrons (collisions) so fission of U-235 is more likely; control rods absorb neutrons, raised/lowered to keep one neutron per fission causing a further fission; coolant carries thermal energy from the core to the heat exchanger and prevents overheating; shielding (steel, thick concrete) absorbs neutrons and gamma rays.',
               'Band A (5–6): at least five points described correctly, in a logical order, with good spelling, punctuation and grammar and correct use of terms.',
               'Band B (3–4): three or four points, mostly clear, with some errors in form and style.',
               'Band C (1–2): one or two points, poorly organised, with weak use of specialist terms.',
               'Band D (0): no relevant physics.'],
          steps: [
            { say: 'Start with the chain reaction: each uranium two three five fission releases two or three neutrons that can cause more fissions.', show: 'chain reaction: $n + {}^{235}\\text{U} \\to$ fission + 2–3 $n$' },
            { say: 'Fast neutrons are unlikely to cause fission, so a moderator such as graphite or water slows them down through collisions.', show: 'moderator: slows neutrons' },
            { say: 'Control rods made of boron or cadmium absorb neutrons, so that on average just one neutron from each fission goes on to cause another.', show: 'control rods: absorb neutrons, steady rate' },
            { say: 'A coolant is pumped through the core to carry the heat away to the boiler and stop the core overheating.', show: 'coolant: removes thermal energy' },
            { say: 'Thick steel and concrete shielding absorbs neutrons and gamma rays to protect people.', show: 'shielding: steel and concrete' },
            { say: 'For top marks, write in full sentences, in a logical order, using these technical terms correctly.', show: 'QWC: logical, accurate, clear' } ] } ] }
  ]
});

